\section{Agency}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what an agency is in legal and business contexts;
  \item describe the steps required to create a valid agency;
  \item list the main duties of agents toward their principals;
  \item list the main duties of principals toward their agents;
  \item explain how liability is allocated between agents and principals;
  \item identify the ways an agency relationship can be terminated.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is an agency?}

An \term{agency} is a relationship in which one person, the \term{agent}, is authorised to act on behalf of another person, the \term{principal}, so that the legal effects of the agent's actions bind the principal. Authority may be express, implied or apparent. Typical examples are a real estate broker selling a house, a lawyer signing a contract for a client, or a student representative negotiating with university administration.

The essential feature is that the principal remains ultimately responsible for the agent's lawful acts. If the agent exceeds authority, the principal may still be bound if a third party reasonably believes the agent had authority.

\subsection{Creating an agency}

Creating a valid agency requires three essential elements:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Consent.} Both principal and agent must agree to the relationship. Consent can be written, oral, or inferred from conduct.
  \item \term{Capacity.} Each party must have legal capacity to contract. A minor, a person lacking mental capacity, or an insolvent company may be unable to create an agency.
  \item \term{Authority.} The principal must intend to give the agent the power to act on the principal's behalf. Authority may be:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \term{Express authority} -- a clear statement, often in a written contract, specifying the scope of the agent's powers.
      \item \term{Implied authority} -- powers necessary to carry out the express authority, such as a manager's power to hire staff.
      \item \term{Apparent authority} -- created when the principal's words or conduct lead a third party to reasonably believe the agent is authorised.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Formal agencies are usually documented in a contract called an \term{agency agreement}. The agreement sets out the scope of authority, duration, remuneration, and termination provisions. Even when no written contract exists, the relationship can be established by conduct, such as a shop owner allowing a clerk to accept payments on his behalf.

\begin{example}
A university hires a recruitment officer to enrol students for a semester. The university signs a written agreement giving the officer authority to sign enrolment forms, collect tuition fees, and issue student IDs. The officer's authority is express because it is stated in the contract. The officer also has implied authority to arrange campus tours, because such activities are necessary to enrol students. If the officer later signs a lease for a new office without written permission, the university may argue the officer lacked authority, unless a third party could reasonably assume the officer had apparent authority based on past conduct.
\end{example}

\subsection{Duties of the agent}

Agents owe several fiduciary duties to their principals:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Duty of loyalty.} The agent must act solely in the principal's best interests and avoid conflicts of interest. The agent may not profit from the agency without the principal's informed consent.
  \item \term{Duty of obedience.} The agent must follow lawful instructions given by the principal and stay within the scope of authority.
  \item \term{Duty of care and skill.} The agent must perform tasks with reasonable competence and diligence, using the level of skill expected of a professional in that field.
  \item \term{Duty to account.} The agent must keep accurate records of all transactions and provide the principal with a full accounting of money received, expenses incurred, and profits earned.
  \item \term{Duty of confidentiality.} The agent must not disclose the principal's confidential information obtained during the agency, unless authorised or required by law.
\end{itemize}

Failure to meet these duties can give the principal a right to sue for breach of fiduciary duty, to recover losses, or to terminate the agency.

\subsection{Duties of the principal}

Principals also have obligations toward their agents:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Payment.} The principal must pay the agreed remuneration, whether a salary, commission, or fee, on time and in the manner stipulated.
  \item \term{Reimbursement.} The principal must reimburse the agent for reasonable expenses incurred while acting on the principal's behalf, provided the expenses are authorised or necessary.
  \item \term{Indemnity.} The principal must protect the agent against liabilities that arise from acting within the scope of authority, including legal costs and damages.
  \item \term{Cooperation.} The principal must provide the information, documents, and resources the agent needs to perform the duties effectively.
\end{itemize}

If a principal breaches these duties, the agent may claim damages, seek restitution, or terminate the agency.

\subsection{Liability of agents and principals}

Liability depends on whether the agent acted within the scope of authority. When an agent acts within authority, the principal is generally liable to third parties for the agent's contractual obligations and torts. The principal may also be liable for any breach of statutory duties that the agent commits in the course of the agency.

If the agent exceeds authority, the principal is not automatically liable. However, the principal may become liable if the third party reasonably believed, based on the principal's conduct, that the agent had authority (apparent authority). In such cases, the principal can seek indemnity from the agent for any loss caused by the unauthorised act.

Agents are personally liable to third parties when they act without authority or exceed it, unless the third party knows the lack of authority. Agents also bear personal liability for breaches of fiduciary duties to their principals.

\begin{example}
A car dealer authorises a sales manager to negotiate purchase prices with customers. The manager, believing he has broad authority, promises a discount that exceeds the limit set in the written agreement. The customer relies on the promise and buys the car. The dealer may be bound by the contract if the customer could reasonably infer the manager had apparent authority to offer such discounts, based on past dealings. If the dealer can show the customer was aware of the limitation, the dealer is not liable, and the manager may be personally liable for any loss the customer suffers.
\end{example}

\subsection{Termination of an agency}

An agency may end in several ways:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Expiration of term.} If the agency agreement specifies a fixed period, the relationship ends automatically when that period expires.
  \item \term{Achievement of purpose.} When the specific task for which the agency was created is completed, the agency terminates.
  \item \term{Mutual agreement.} The principal and agent can agree to end the relationship at any time, provided any contractual notice requirements are met.
  \item \term{Revocation by the principal.} A principal may withdraw authority, but revocation is ineffective if the agent has already acted within authority and the third party relied on that authority.
  \item \term{Renunciation by the agent.} An agent may quit, but must give reasonable notice and may remain liable for obligations incurred before resignation.
  \item \term{Operation of law.} Death, bankruptcy, or loss of capacity of either party automatically ends the agency.
\end{itemize}

Termination does not relieve the principal of liability for acts performed while the agency was in force, nor does it relieve the agent of liability for breaches that occurred before termination.

\begin{example}
A marketing firm appoints a freelance designer to create a brand identity for a client. The contract states the project will be completed in three months. After two months the client decides to cancel the project and informs the designer. Because the purpose of the agency (the design work) has not been achieved, the client must give the notice required in the agreement. If the designer has already delivered preliminary sketches that the client has used, the client remains liable for those uses even after termination.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} To decide whether an agency exists, ask: (1) Did the principal give the agent authority to act on the principal's behalf? (2) Did the agent accept that authority? (3) Is there evidence of consent, capacity, and a lawful purpose? If the answers are yes, an agency relationship is likely.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Agency & Relationship where an agent acts on behalf of a principal. \\
Principal & The person who authorises another to act for them. \\
Agent & The person authorised to act for the principal. \\
Express authority & Authority given directly, usually in writing. \\
Implied authority & Authority inferred as necessary to carry out express authority. \\
Apparent authority & Authority that a third party reasonably believes exists based on the principal's conduct. \\
Duty of loyalty & Agent must act only in the principal's interests. \\
Duty of obedience & Agent must follow the principal's lawful instructions. \\
Duty of care and skill & Agent must perform tasks competently. \\
Duty to account & Agent must keep and provide accurate financial records. \\
Duty of confidentiality & Agent must protect the principal's private information. \\
Indemnity & Principal's obligation to cover the agent's liabilities incurred within authority. \\
Liability of principal & Principal is bound for acts of an agent within authority or apparent authority. \\
Liability of agent & Agent is personally liable for unauthorised acts and breaches of fiduciary duty. \\
Termination of agency & Ways an agency relationship can end, such as expiry, mutual agreement, revocation, renunciation, or operation of law. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Companies}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define incorporation and describe each step required to create a company;
  \item explain the principal duties that directors owe to the company and how those duties are enforced;
  \item identify the core rights of shareholders and illustrate how those rights are exercised in practice;
  \item understand the purpose of a company constitution and how it structures the relationship between directors and shareholders;
  \item describe the function of a board committee and how it supports corporate governance.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Incorporation}

\term{Incorporation} is the legal process by which a group of people creates a separate legal entity called a company. Once incorporated, the company can own property, sue and be sued, and continue its existence even if the original founders leave.

The typical steps are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Choose a name.} The name must be unique and include a legal suffix such as Ltd or PLC.
  \item \term{Prepare a memorandum of association.} This document sets out the company name, its registered office, its objects and the amount of share capital.
  \item \term{Prepare articles of association.} The articles are a contract between the company and its members that governs internal management, voting rights and the powers of directors.
  \item \term{File the incorporation documents.} The memorandum, articles and a registration form are lodged with the corporate regulator.
  \item \term{Pay the registration fee.} The regulator issues a certificate of incorporation once the fee is received and the documents are accepted.
  \item \term{Issue shares.} The founders subscribe for the initial share capital and share certificates are issued to record ownership.
\end{enumerate}

After incorporation the company becomes a \term{legal person}. It can open bank accounts, borrow money and enter contracts in its own name. The separation between the company and its owners is called \term{limited liability}; shareholders are liable only for the amount they have paid for their shares.

\begin{example}
A team of three engineers decides to launch a software start up. They select the name TechNova Ltd, draft a memorandum stating that the company will develop cloud based applications, and adopt standard model articles. They file the documents with the regulator, pay the twelve dollar fee, and receive a certificate of incorporation. Each engineer subscribes for one thousand shares at one dollar per share, receives share certificates, and the company now exists as a separate legal entity.
\end{example}

\subsection{Directors' duties}

Directors are the people appointed to manage the company on a day to day basis. The law imposes several fiduciary duties on them. The most important duties are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Duty of care, skill and diligence.} Directors must act with the competence and attention that a reasonably diligent person would use in the same position. They must read reports, understand the business and seek professional advice when needed.
  \item \term{Duty to act in good faith in the best interests of the company.} Directors must place the company's interests above their own personal interests. This includes avoiding conflicts of interest and not using corporate opportunities for personal gain.
  \item \term{Duty to avoid conflicts of interest.} If a director has a personal interest in a transaction, they must disclose it to the board and, where required, obtain approval from disinterested directors or shareholders.
  \item \term{Duty not to accept benefits from third parties.} A director may not accept a gift, loan or other benefit that could influence their judgment, unless the benefit is approved by the board.
  \item \term{Duty to comply with the law.} Directors must ensure the company complies with statutory obligations such as filing annual accounts, maintaining proper books, and paying taxes.
\end{itemize}

Breaches of these duties can lead to personal liability. A court may order a director to compensate the company, disqualify them from acting as a director, or impose fines.

\begin{example}
The finance director of a manufacturing firm learns that a supplier is offering a five percent discount if the firm purchases raw material through a company owned by the director's brother. The director discloses the relationship to the board, obtains written approval, and records the transaction in the minutes. By following the disclosure and approval process, the director satisfies the duty to avoid conflicts of interest.
\end{example}

\subsection{Shareholders' rights}

Shareholders are the owners of a company. Their rights are defined by statute, the articles of association and any shareholders' agreement. Core rights include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Right to receive dividends.} When the board declares a dividend, each shareholder receives a proportionate share based on the number of shares held.
  \item \term{Right to vote at general meetings.} Each ordinary share usually carries one vote. Shareholders can vote on matters such as the appointment or removal of directors, changes to the articles, and approval of major transactions.
  \item \term{Right to receive information.} Shareholders are entitled to receive annual reports, financial statements and notices of meetings. They may also request copies of certain company records.
  \item \term{Pre emptive right to subscribe for new shares.} When a company issues additional shares, existing shareholders often have a right to purchase a proportionate number of the new shares before they are offered to outsiders, protecting their ownership percentage.
  \item \term{Right to bring a derivative action.} If the directors breach their duties and the company suffers loss, a shareholder may bring a lawsuit on behalf of the company, subject to court approval.
\end{itemize}

Shareholders exercise these rights primarily through the annual general meeting and any extraordinary general meetings called for specific purposes. Votes are usually cast in person, by proxy, or electronically, depending on the company's procedures.

\subsection{Company constitution}

The \term{company constitution} is the combination of the memorandum of association and the articles of association. It sets out the rules that govern the relationship between the company, its directors and its shareholders. The constitution can be customised, but certain provisions are mandatory, such as the requirement to keep a register of members and the procedure for calling a general meeting.

Key elements of a constitution include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Share capital structure.} Defines the classes of shares, their nominal value and any special rights attached to particular classes.
  \item \term{Director appointment and removal.} Specifies how directors are appointed, the length of their term and the process for removing a director.
  \item \term{Decision making procedures.} Sets the quorum for meetings, the majority required for ordinary and special resolutions, and the method of voting.
  \item \term{Dividend policy.} May include rules on how and when profits can be distributed to shareholders.
  \item \term{Alteration of the constitution.} Describes the procedure for amending the memorandum or articles, usually requiring a special resolution.
\end{itemize}

A well drafted constitution helps prevent disputes by clearly allocating powers and expectations. It also provides a framework for enforcing directors' duties and shareholders' rights.

\begin{example}
Beta Ltd wishes to create a new class of shares that carry ten votes per share and a fixed dividend of three percent. The board drafts an amendment to the articles that defines the new class, sets the voting weight, and specifies the dividend formula. The amendment is put to a special resolution at the annual general meeting, obtains the required ninety percent approval, and is filed with the regulator. The new class is now part of the company constitution and can be issued to investors.
\end{example}

\subsection{Board committees and corporate governance}

A board committee is a smaller group of directors delegated to focus on a specific area such as audit, remuneration or risk. Committees allow detailed scrutiny of complex matters and provide recommendations to the full board. Their existence is usually required by the constitution or by best practice guidelines.

Key points about committees:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Composition.} Members are appointed from among the directors, often with a mix of executive and non executive directors to ensure independence.
  \item \term{Authority.} The constitution may grant a committee limited powers, such as approving audit plans, but major decisions still require full board approval.
  \item \term{Reporting.} Committees must keep minutes and report their findings to the board, creating a transparent record of deliberations.
  \item \term{Accountability.} Directors remain ultimately responsible for the company's actions, even when tasks are delegated to a committee.
\end{itemize}

Effective use of committees enhances corporate governance by improving oversight, reducing the burden on the full board, and ensuring that specialist expertise is applied to critical areas.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} A group of individuals wants to start a business that will raise money from many investors, limit personal liability, and have a formal governance structure. Do they need to incorporate? If they do, will the directors they appoint be subject to fiduciary duties, and will the investors receive shareholder rights such as voting and dividend entitlement? Answer: Yes, incorporation creates a company, directors acquire duties, and investors become shareholders with the listed rights.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List the six steps required to incorporate a company and explain why each step is necessary.
  \item A director discovers that a contract under negotiation would benefit a business owned by his spouse. Describe the procedure the director should follow to comply with the duty to avoid conflicts of interest.
  \item Explain the difference between a dividend right and a voting right. Give an example of a situation where a shareholder might exercise each right.
  \item A company proposes to issue new shares to raise capital. What pre emptive right do existing shareholders have, and how can the company lawfully override that right?
  \item Beta Ltd wants to introduce a new class of shares with special voting rights. Outline the constitutional steps required to create and issue those shares.
  \item Identify two advantages of having an audit committee and describe how its reports contribute to board oversight.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Incorporation & The legal process of creating a separate corporate entity. \\
Legal person & An entity that can own property, sue and be sued in its own name. \\
Limited liability & Shareholders are liable only for the amount they have paid for their shares. \\
Directors & Individuals appointed to manage the company's affairs. \\
Duty of care, skill and diligence & Obligation to act with reasonable competence and attention. \\
Duty to act in good faith & Obligation to place the company's interests above personal interests. \\
Conflict of interest & A situation where a director's personal interest could influence corporate decisions. \\
Shareholder & An owner of a company who holds shares. \\
Dividend & A distribution of profit to shareholders, proportionate to shareholding. \\
Pre emptive right & The right of existing shareholders to purchase new shares before outsiders. \\
Derivative action & A lawsuit brought by a shareholder on behalf of the company. \\
Company constitution & The combined memorandum and articles that set out the rules governing the company. \\
Special resolution & A resolution that requires a higher majority, usually ninety percent, to amend the constitution. \\
Board committee & A sub group of directors tasked with detailed oversight of a specific area. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

